<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahaz was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the second half of the 8th century BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a> attacked him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syro-ephraimite-war">Syro-Ephraimite War</a>, he appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> and became an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, a submission recorded in Assyrian inscriptions under his full name, Jehoahaz. The Bible condemns him for introducing foreign cults, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaz.jpg" alt="Ahaz" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahaz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahaz was the twelfth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and the son and successor of Jotham. His name is an abbreviation of Jehoahaz, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> has held. According to the Second Book of Kings he was 20 when he became king and reigned 16 years. The dates are disputed: Edwin R. Thiele placed a co-regency with Jotham from 736/735 BCE and a sole reign from 732/731 to 716/715 BCE, while William F. Albright dated his reign to 744 to 728 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At his accession Ahaz faced a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a>, who wished to compel him to join them in opposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> under Tiglath-Pileser III. The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> relates that the prophet counselled him to trust in God rather than foreign allies; Ahaz refused to ask for a sign, and Isaiah foretold the birth of a child to be called Immanuel. Ahaz instead called in the Assyrians. Tiglath-Pileser sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and annexed Aram, and then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and other territory of Israel, deporting its people, as his own inscription also records. The campaign lasted two years, 734 to 732 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was relieved of its neighbours, but its protector claimed suzerainty over it. In 732 Ahaz went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to swear homage to Tiglath-Pileser, and having admired an altar there he had a copy made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and made it a permanent part of Temple worship, with changes to the Temple's furniture. The biblical writers also say he offered his son by fire, or made him pass through fire; the words may describe a purification or a sacrifice. The Deuteronomistic historian judged his rule disastrous for the country's religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ahaz died at the age of 36 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>, whose reforms were largely aimed at undoing what Ahaz had done. According to 2 Chronicles, he was not laid in the tombs of the kings. Two inscribed objects name him. A clay seal impression reads Belonging to Ahaz, son of Yehotam, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, and most scholars think it authentic; a carnelian seal of the 8th century BCE, now at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a>, belonged to Ushna, servant of Ahaz. The annals of Tiglath-Pileser III also record tribute from Ahaz of Judah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pekah instead joined Rezin of Damascus in an anti-Assyrian alliance and attacked Judah when its king <b>Ahaz</b> refused to take part, the conflict known as the Syro-Ephraimite War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Judah was repeatedly a vassal: <b>Ahaz</b> submitted to Tiglath-pileser III of Assyria around 734 BCE, and Manasseh, who ruled for about half a century, remained a loyal Assyrian tributary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
